--- Finding (executable_library): a module's top-level value that is not a
--- scalar literal — a call (`four = double[2]`) or a list literal (`primes =
--- [2, 3, 5]`) — makes every program importing the module fail to build
--- compiled, whether the program reads the value or not. Needs
--- executable_library_1_lib.co beside it.
---
--- Interpreted (`crow run -i`): 6, then [2, 3, 5] — the module's values are
--- computed as the program starts (a `log` in a function a value calls prints).
--- Compiled (`crow run`):
---   error[E0010]: allocations are not allowed in constants
---   (with the list gone from the module: error[E0015]: cannot call non-const
---   function `executable_library_1_lib_double` in constants)
---   error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused
---   what Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program
--- Expected: 6 and [2, 3, 5] in both modes. The expansion keeps a module's
--- top-level assignments as prefixed values (main.rs, "Module-level variables
--- become prefixed assigns"), and the emitter prints each as a Rust `const`
--- (emit_rs/program.rs emit_module_consts), which holds only a constant
--- expression: a scalar literal (`pi = 3.14`) builds, a vec!, a String built at
--- run time or a call of the module's function does not. Dropping the read of
--- `primes` does not help: the const is printed anyway.
use executable_library_1_lib

log[executable_library_1_lib.double[3]]
log[executable_library_1_lib.primes]
